WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.770
A Java program organizes behavior into classes and methods.

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:08.701
Variables have declared types, and the compiler checks many mistakes before the

00:00:08.701 --> 00:00:09.498
program runs.

00:00:09.648 --> 00:00:13.842
Primitive types represent values such as integers and booleans; reference types refer

00:00:13.842 --> 00:00:15.939
to objects and may be null.

00:00:16.089 --> 00:00:19.719
Choose types that express the domain instead of passing every value as

00:00:19.719 --> 00:00:20.325
a string.

00:00:20.475 --> 00:00:25.462
For commerce calculations, decide whether money is represented as integer minor units

00:00:25.462 --> 00:00:28.371
or BigDecimal with an explicit rounding policy.

00:00:28.521 --> 00:00:32.752
Binary floating-point cannot exactly represent many decimal fractions.

00:00:32.902 --> 00:00:37.221
Construct BigDecimal from decimal strings when exact decimal input matters.

00:00:37.371 --> 00:00:41.465
Validate quantities and prices at the domain boundary, not only in a

00:00:41.465 --> 00:00:42.147
web form.

00:00:42.297 --> 00:00:47.179
JavaBasics.Line contains a stock-keeping unit, quantity, and unit price.

00:00:47.329 --> 00:00:52.504
Its constructor rejects an empty SKU, nonpositive quantity, or negative price.

00:00:52.654 --> 00:00:58.670
Three items priced at 0.10 produce exactly 0.30 in the supplied test.

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:03.090
A valid domain object makes later calculations easier to reason about.

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:06.801
Implement subtotal, tax, and total for a basket.

00:01:06.951 --> 00:01:11.416
Choose a rounding rule and test zero items, a fractional tax, a

00:01:11.416 --> 00:01:13.277
large quantity, and invalid input.

00:01:13.427 --> 00:01:15.203
Compile with Java 21.

00:01:15.353 --> 00:01:18.394
Keep arithmetic separate from printing and HTTP.

00:01:18.544 --> 00:01:22.547
State when rounding occurs, such as once per invoice or once per

00:01:22.547 --> 00:01:24.548
line, because the totals can differ.

00:01:24.698 --> 00:01:28.460
Test expected decimal values rather than formatted strings alone.

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:29.282
Question.

00:01:29.432 --> 00:01:32.406
Why not use double for exact currency arithmetic?

00:01:32.556 --> 00:01:33.133
Answer.

00:01:33.283 --> 00:01:38.095
Many decimal amounts cannot be represented exactly in binary floating-point; use minor

00:01:38.095 --> 00:01:41.704
units or decimal arithmetic with a stated rounding rule.

00:01:41.854 --> 00:01:42.526
Question.

00:01:42.676 --> 00:01:45.802
What is the difference between a compile error and an exception?

00:01:45.952 --> 00:01:46.528
Answer.

00:01:46.678 --> 00:01:51.674
A compile error prevents valid bytecode generation; an exception occurs while a

00:01:51.674 --> 00:01:53.339
compiled program is executing.
